ething ships late.=0A=0A01The Shift=0A=0A=0ATwo Companies, Two Answers=0A=
=0ABoth diagnosed before deciding. For one, the answer was a new system; fo=
r the=0Aother, fixing the one they had.=0A=0ATwo companies had operating pr=
oblems. Both looked at how work actually moved=0Abefore they spent money. T=
hey got different answers.=0A=0AGutchess Lumber was running on several prog=
rams plus manual spreadsheets, and=0Akeeping the data right took constant o=
versight. After mapping the process, the=0Ateam decided the system really w=
as the problem. They chose a new ERP and rolled=0Ait out with minimal disru=
ption to production. Their IT director says the payoff=0Ahas been less rewo=
rk and better reporting.=0A=0AStainless Works had an ERP that was feeding t=
he floor wrong information. They=0Adidn=E2=80=99t replace it. They correcte=
d the routings and set up their work centers for=0Ascheduling, and lead tim=
e dropped from 12 to 14 weeks to five.=0A=0ASame starting point, opposite a=
nswers. The diagnosis decided it, not the request=0Afor new software.=0A=0A=
Check for a broken handoff, bad data or an unused feature before you blame =
the=0Asystem.=0A=0ABoth stories come from NIST MEP case studies.=0A=0A02Fro=
m the Floor=0A=0A=0AWhat to Check Before You Sign=0A=0AKirsten Austin [http=
s://www.linkedin.com/in/kirstenaustin/], President of DCSC=0AInc. [https://=
www.dcsccorp.com/], sells technology into operations like yours.=0ASome of =
her most useful advice to buyers is to hold off.=0A=0ASometimes they're not=
 ready for the technology that we have. It's time for them=0Ato double back=
.=0A=0AKirsten Austin=0A=0APresident, DCSC Inc. The Operations Brief Round=
table No. 2, October 2, 2026=0A[https://www.theoperationsbrief.com/roundtab=
le/rt02-worth-the-investment/]=0A=0AHer point: before you sign, look at you=
r data, your floor layout and who owns=0Aeach handoff. Get those right and =
whatever you buy later has a fair shot. That=E2=80=99s=0Aone vendor=E2=80=
=99s view, and it matches what Stainless Works found.=0A=0ARead the full Ro=
undtable No. 2 recap =E2=86=92=0A[https://www.theoperationsbrief.com/roundt=
able/rt02-worth-the-investment/]=0A=0A03The Stack=0A=0A=0ARun the Check You=
rself, or Let the Skill Do It=0A=0AA manual check you can run this week, an=
d a skill that runs it for you.=0A=0ATry this before your next software req=
uest.=0A=0APick your last three orders that shipped late or wrong. Trace ea=
ch one from=0Aorder entry to the dock and mark where it stalled.=0A=0AThen =
ask: was the information wrong, did it not get passed along, was there a=0A=
feature you already have that nobody used, or could the system truly not do=
 it=3F=0A=0AIf most of your answers land in the first three, new software w=
on=E2=80=99t fix it.=0A=0AOr let the Purchase Verdict Skill do it.=0A=0AGiv=
e it one order that went wrong, the system records for that order (pull the=
m=0Abefore your system clears them out) and a few similar orders that went =
right.=0A=0AYou get back a one-page answer: system problem or process probl=
em. If it can=E2=80=99t=0Atell yet, it says what=E2=80=99s missing and who =
needs to find it.=0A=0AWorks with Claude. Setup steps for Claude and ChatGP=
T are on the download page.=0A=0ADownload the Purchase Verdict Skill=0A[htt=
